Modern Vaccination: Principles and Practice\par
\par
Vaccination is one of the greatest achievements of modern medicine. It has eliminated smallpox, nearly eradicated polio, and dramatically reduced the burden of measles, tetanus, and many other infectious diseases. The principle behind vaccination is simple: by exposing the immune system to a harmless form of a pathogen, we prepare it to fight the real infection.\par
\par
There are several types of vaccines. Live attenuated vaccines use weakened forms of the virus or bacterium and produce a strong, long lasting immune response. Inactivated vaccines use killed pathogens and often require booster doses. Subunit vaccines contain only specific proteins or polysaccharides, which reduces the risk of side effects. Messenger RNA vaccines are a recent development and have proven highly effective against COVID-19.\par
\par
Vaccination programs are organized at the national and international level. The World Health Organization provides guidelines, coordinates supply chains, and monitors safety. Each country develops its own immunization schedule based on local epidemiology and available resources. Common childhood vaccines include those against measles, mumps, rubella, diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis, and hepatitis B.\par
\par
Public confidence in vaccines is essential for the success of immunization programs. Misinformation and fear can lead to declining coverage and outbreaks of preventable diseases. Health authorities must communicate clearly and honestly about both benefits and rare risks. Community engagement and education play a crucial role in maintaining trust.\par
